1. Design a Redis-based caching mechanism for sample responses
2. Create a cache key based on organization description or industry type
3. Set appropriate TTL (time-to-live) for cached responses (e.g., 24 hours)
4. Implement cache check before generating new responses
5. Add cache update logic for new organization types
6. Sample implementation:
```typescript
async function getCachedOrGenerateResponses(organizationDescription: string) {
  const cacheKey = `sample_responses:${createHash(organizationDescription)}`;

  // Try to get from cache first
  const cachedResponses = await redis.get(cacheKey);
  if (cachedResponses) return JSON.parse(cachedResponses);

  // Generate new responses if not in cache
  const generatedResponses = await generateAIResponses(organizationDescription);

  // Cache the new responses
  await redis.set(cacheKey, JSON.stringify(generatedResponses), 'EX', 86400); // 24 hours

  return generatedResponses;
}
```

1. Identify potential failure points in the system
2. Design fallback responses for API failures
3. Implement error boundary components in the React frontend
4. Create a set of generic industry-agnostic responses as ultimate fallbacks
5. Add logging for all fallback activations to monitor frequency
6. Implement progressive degradation rather than complete failure
7. Sample implementation:
```typescript
async function getSampleResponses(organizationDescription: string) {
  try {
    // Primary path - dynamic AI-generated responses
    return await getCachedOrGenerateResponses(organizationDescription);
  } catch (error) {
    // Log the error
    console.error('Failed to generate dynamic responses:', error);

    // Fallback to generic responses
    return [
      "Hi, I'm interested in learning more about your services",
      "Do you offer consultations?",
      "What makes your company different from competitors?",
      "How can I schedule an appointment?"
    ];
  }
}
```
